Keep the part of a row a scroll leaves over.

Surface.scrollCallback keeps `poff - amount * cell_size` as the scroll still
to come, but divides without truncating first, so `amount` is fractional and
the remainder always zero: every scroll that crosses a row drops the rest of
it, and a slow trackpad scroll moves less than the fingers did. Truncating
first keeps it. The pin still divides untruncated.

From libghostty-spm's Patches/ghostty/0010-fix-scroll-remainder-zeroing.patch (MIT, 2026 @Lakr233),
cut as a `git diff` against the ThirdParty/ghostty pin.

diff --git a/src/Surface.zig b/src/Surface.zig
index 22e4ae413..de3a8de2f 100644
--- a/src/Surface.zig
+++ b/src/Surface.zig
@@ -3603,7 +3603,7 @@ pub fn scrollCallback(
         }
 
         // We scroll by the number of rows in the offset and save the remainder
-        const amount = poff / cell_size;
+        const amount = @trunc(poff / cell_size);
         assert(@abs(amount) >= 1);
         self.mouse.pending_scroll_y = poff - (amount * cell_size);
 
@@ -3628,7 +3628,7 @@ pub fn scrollCallback(
             break :x .{};
         }
 
-        const amount = poff / cell_size;
+        const amount = @trunc(poff / cell_size);
         assert(@abs(amount) >= 1);
         self.mouse.pending_scroll_x = poff - (amount * cell_size);
         const delta: isize = @intFromFloat(@trunc(amount));
